  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a literature review of 45 existing peer-reviewed studies rather than new empirical fieldwork.",
      "what": "This paper synthesizes 45 peer-reviewed studies on gentrification in African cities, analyzing how land commodification, rent gap dynamics, gentrification actors, and displacement manifest in these contexts. It identifies that land commodification is propelled by rapid urbanization, foreign capital, and shifting land tenure, while rent gaps frequently arise from under-utilized state lands and ecological commodification rather than classic urban disinvestment.",
      "why": "It challenges the universal applicability of Global North gentrification theories by demonstrating that African gentrification relies on heterogeneous multi-actor coalitions rather than standard developer-led models, and involves displacement that is ecological and socio-economic rather than just physical."
    }
